%T A401511 29,35,35,35,37,38,40,41,44,42,42,40,42,47,47,48,57,56,59,57,58,60,65,
%U A401511 68,68,68,69,73,77,78,79,76,78,77,78,79,94,94,95,94,97,96,100
%N A401511 Value of m - (2k + 3), where m is the prime index of the n-th prime that is the sum of three consecutive primes (A034962), and k is the prime index of the first of those three consecutive primes (A073681).
%C A401511 Runs of identical values appear (e.g., three 35s at n=30, four 149s at n=105, and five 7653s at n=6495). Question: is there a bound or limit?
%H A401511 Martin Michael Musatov, <a href="/A401511/b401511.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%e A401511 a(1) = 0 because the 1st prime that is the sum of three consecutive primes is 23 (index m=9), and the three consecutive primes are 5, 7, and 11, where 5 has prime index k=3, the formula gives m - (2k + 3) = 9 - (2*3 + 3) = 9 - 9 = 0.
%e A401511 a(5) = 1 because the 5th prime that is the sum of three consecutive primes is 71 (index m=20), and the three consecutive primes are 19, 23, and 29, where 19 has prime index k=8, the formula gives m - (2k + 3) = 20 - (2*8 + 3) = 20 - 19 = 1.
%Y A401511 Cf. A000040, A034961, A034962, A073681.
%K A401511 nonn,new
